      "raw": "After analyzing all three views, we observe that each view contains the same three garments: a pair of dark gray shorts, a red-and-white striped t-shirt, and a pair of black cargo pants. The garments are shown in different arrangements across the views, but their visual characteristics (fabric, color, construction) remain consistent.\n\nEach view provides a set of mask regions (R001\u2013R003, R004\u2013R006, R007\u2013R009) corresponding to these three garments. The regions are labeled consistently across views: R001/R004/R007 for the shorts, R002/R005/R008 for the t-shirt, and R003/R006/R009 for the pants.\n\nSince each garment appears in every view and is represented by a unique region ID in each view, we can assign each garment to a track using the corresponding region IDs from each view.\n\nThus, the three garments are:\n\n- **Dark gray shorts**: Appears in R001 (v2), R004 (v1), R007 (v0)\n- **Red-and-white striped t-shirt**: Appears in R002 (v2), R005 (v1), R008 (v0)\n- **Black cargo pants**: Appears in R003 (v2), R006 (v1), R009 (v0)\n\nNo garment is missing from any view, and no region is shared between garments. All regions are uniquely assigned to one garment per view.\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tracks\": [\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"dark gray shorts\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R001\", \"R004\", \"R007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"red-and-white striped t-shirt\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R002\", \"R005\", \"R008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"black cargo pants\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R003\", \"R006\", \"R009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
